  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "A system of equations may have more than one solution. Consider the following system of equations\n$$\\begin{aligned}2y-3x&=1\\\\3x-2y&=-1.\\end{aligned}$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p21-q2-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $(x,y)=(3,5)$ and $(x,y)=(1,2)$ are both solutions of this system of equations.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Both $(3,5)$ and $(1,2)$ satisfy both equations.",
          "worked": "For $(x,y)=(3,5)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2y-3x&=2(5)-3(3)\\\\2y-3x&=1\\\\3x-2y&=3(3)-2(5)\\\\3x-2y&=-1.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $(x,y)=(1,2)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2y-3x&=2(2)-3(1)\\\\2y-3x&=1\\\\3x-2y&=3(1)-2(2)\\\\3x-2y&=-1.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth pairs satisfy both equations, so both are solutions.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that for any real $t$, $(x,y)=\\left(t,\\frac12+\\frac32t\\right)$ is a solution of this system of equations.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Substitution gives $2y-3x=1$ and $3x-2y=-1$ for every real $t$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $x=t$ and $y=\\frac12+\\frac32t$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2y-3x&=2\\left(\\frac12+\\frac32t\\right)-3t\\\\2y-3x&=1+3t-3t\\\\2y-3x&=1\\\\3x-2y&=3t-2\\left(\\frac12+\\frac32t\\right)\\\\3x-2y&=3t-1-3t\\\\3x-2y&=-1.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth equations hold for every real $t$, so the given pair is a solution.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      }
    ]
  },
